WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.012
Module 13 is the device mesh — letting two machines you own work together.

2
00:00:03.012 --> 00:00:07.000
This episode: turn it on, find each other, and pair them with a real proof-of-knowledge handshake.

3
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:08.040
Settings, Devices.

4
00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:14.283
Give this machine a name, hit Turn on, and optionally set it to start with the app so it's always reachable.

5
00:00:14.283 --> 00:00:17.000
That's the whole setup on one side of the link.

6
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:20.404
On the same LAN, machines with the mesh on find each other automatically.

7
00:00:20.404 --> 00:00:26.000
If discovery doesn't show one — different subnet, different network — you can always add it directly by address instead.

8
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:30.339
Pairing: on the target machine, click Show pairing code — it's valid five minutes and single-use.

9
00:00:30.339 --> 00:00:33.022
On the initiator, enter that code plus the target's address.

10
00:00:33.022 --> 00:00:40.000
Behind the scenes it's a proof-of-knowledge handshake, so neither the short code nor the long-lived token it produces ever crosses the network in the clear.

11
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:45.637
Once that handshake completes, the two machines have a long-lived trust relationship — you don't re-pair every session.

12
00:00:45.637 --> 00:00:49.000
What that trust does and doesn't grant is next episode's whole subject.

13
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:52.149
Say this one loud and clear: the mesh traffic itself is not encrypted yet.

14
00:00:52.149 --> 00:00:58.957
The handshake protects your credentials and blocks replay attacks, but everything after that — prompts, tool output, retrieved documents — travels in plaintext.

15
00:00:58.957 --> 00:01:00.489
That's fine on a home LAN you trust.

16
00:01:00.489 --> 00:01:05.000
Across the open internet, only inside a VPN or tunnel, Tailscale, WireGuard, SSH — never a forwarded port.

17
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:05.935
Two machines are paired.

18
00:01:05.935 --> 00:01:11.000
Next: exactly what that pairing lets one machine do on the other — and the hard boundaries that hold no matter what you switch on.

